  "worked_solution": "First, let's simplify the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+x & >x-2 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+x-x & >x-2-x \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & >-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use either of two methods to solve the inequality.\n\n**Method 1 - Solving an Absolute Value Inequality**\n\nWe know that the square of *any* real number is greater than or equal to zero.\n\nSo, the inequality is true for every real number. Therefore the solution is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\n**Method 2 - Picturing a Quadratic Curve**\n\nStarting from $3{x}^{2}>-2$ and moving all terms to the left-hand side, we reach\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}+2>0.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding parabola given by $\\,y=3{x}^{2}+2.$ :\n\nSince the leading coefficient of $3{x}^{2}+2$   is positive, the parabola opens upward.  The desired inequality $(y>0)$ is always satisfied. Therefore, the solution set to the given inequality includes all real numbers. That is, $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13965374/assets/39e923bcf71c650441cbe39bd173056a008e5926679271109df479ca76bd8294.png)",
